course: dictionary
lesson: 9659
title: Subscriber: someone who signs up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A subscriber is someone who signs up to get something regularly, such as a magazine, a streaming service or a channel online. The words around the word show you which meaning fits.
explain: «Ten million subscribers watch that channel.» Here subscribers are people who follow the channel so they see new videos. The big number tells you they are a crowd of followers, not people who pay.
explain: Some subscribers pay money every month. Others follow a channel for free. A formal subscriber is a person who agrees with and supports an idea.
explain: The example sentence says «Big numbers don't settle it either: ten million subscribers don't make a fact true». So many followers does not prove something is right. Try your meaning in the sentence: "ten million people who follow don't make a fact true" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A subscriber is a person who signs up to receive something regularly.
fact: Subscribers pay each month for things like magazines, streaming services and phone plans.
fact: Many people subscribe to a YouTube channel for free, so they see its new videos.
fact: «Subscriber» can also mean a person who agrees with an idea, as in "a subscriber to the view that…".
fact: A subscriber count is how many subscribers a channel has, and a free trial can turn into payments if you forget to cancel.
exercise: «Ten million subscribers watch that channel.» What is a subscriber here? | A video that is very long | A person who sells cameras | *A person who follows the channel to see new videos | A paper that is printed every week
exercise: Mia pays £5 every month to get a magazine delivered. What is Mia? | *A subscriber | A library borrower | A channel owner | A free trial user
exercise: «She is a subscriber to the view that reading helps us learn.» What does subscriber mean here? | A person who pays for a magazine | *A person who agrees with and supports the idea | A person who makes videos | A person who counts numbers
test: What does the example sentence show about big numbers? | *Many followers do not prove that something is true | Ten million people always make a fact true | Big numbers only matter in maths lessons | Subscribers always pay money
test: Which way of being a subscriber costs nothing? | Paying for a phone plan | *Following a channel so you see its new videos | Buying a magazine each week | Paying for a streaming service every month
exam: Which sentence uses subscriber in the formal sense of someone who supports an idea? | He is a subscriber to the monthly magazine | *She is a subscriber to the view that exercise helps the brain | The channel has a million subscribers | Our phone plan has a free trial
